WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:04.320
Bloopers are just a part of doing business
when that business is show business.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.861
Actors are bound to mess up a line every now
and then, or, after a long day on set, improvise

00:00:08.861 --> 00:00:10.379
and make their co-stars laugh.

00:00:10.379 --> 00:00:14.320
While most of these flubs and goofs are innocent,
some are downright raunchy.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:15.480
For example...

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.619
Full House

00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:19.600
Has there ever been a more innocuous TV show
than Full House?

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.370
The often sickeningly-sweet sitcom used its
"awwwwwww" track almost as often as its laugh

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:25.370
track.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:29.770
But two of its cast members were veteran stand-ups,
both quick on their feet… and even occasionally

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:30.990
pretty filthy.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:35.840
In this blooper, Dave Coulier really lets
Bob Saget have it, but apologizes to another

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:36.840
character, off-camera…

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.740
"I can really see how little your d--- is
in that suit."

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:42.740
"Sorry, Patty."

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:44.739
"'Sorry, Patty?'

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:46.680
How about, 'Sorry Bob?'

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.880
'Bob's got a real small weiner!

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.040
Sorry, Patty.'"

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.540
Friends

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.510
The friends of Friends were, well, friends.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:58.170
And when people are that close, both in friendship
and proximity, there's bound to be the occasional

00:00:58.170 --> 00:00:59.850
bit of accidental nudity.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:03.969
It's even a plot line that was actually scripted
on Friends at least a couple of times — but

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:05.970
this is not one of those times…

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:10.290
"One hand on the sheet, Joe.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.130
"You saw it didn't you?"

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:13.240
"Yeah, I saw it."

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:14.850
The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:18.940
In this blooper from the otherwise classy
sitcom The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air, Carlton

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:22.390
is supposed to be concentrating really hard
— but he looks like he's doing something

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:25.400
else … so somebody in the sound booth "sweetened"
the shot …

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:27.140
"Go on, try it."

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.540
The Soupy Sales Show

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:39.330
Soupy was a beloved kiddie TV host in the
early days of TV.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:43.060
But one day, his crew surprised him in the
middle of a taping with an R-rated special

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.760
guest behind an on-set door …

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:52.860
"Beautiful!"

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:53.980
"Get out of here."

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.180
"Right now, let's have a couple of commercials."

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.810
30 Rock

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:07.530
Will Arnett had a recurring role on 30 Rock
as Devon Banks, a nefarious and sometimes

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.790
predatory NBC executive with his eye on the
innocent page, Kenneth.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:16.010
His "flirting" could get a little creepy and
alarming, such as when he force-fed Kenneth

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:19.220
a bottle of apple juice, and Arnett's riffing
got a little out-of-hand ...

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.080
"God your mouth is so thirsty.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.480
Such a thirsty mouth."

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:26.480
Better Off Ted

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:31.500
This quirky ABC sitcom aired for just two
seasons, but it quickly became a cult hit

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.290
for its surreal and twisted take on office
culture.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:38.940
In one episode, a typo in a memo requires,
rather than discourages, employees of the

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.200
show's workplace to use "offensive or insulting"
language.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.400
This led to some pretty insane outtakes as
the actors and writers kept trying to one-up

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.400
each other.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.400
Here's just a sample …

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:49.900
"I'm terrible at insults."

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.050
"I can help you, you mouse-humping piece of
s---."

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:57.040
"I can help you, you sweaty thong in a hooker's
money crack."

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.060
"Could you repeat that in English?

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:01.150
I don't speak the language of the square-jawed
c-- puppets."

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:02.750
Parks and Recreation

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:07.050
A tremendous cast of comedy all-stars kept
things loose and improvised from time to time

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:11.400
on the set of NBC's small-town government
comedy, Parks and Recreation.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.980
But Chris Pratt, in particular, had a difficult
time not crossing the line, such as in this

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:19.650
scene where the gang pretends to be old people
to better empathize with them ...

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:23.840
"When I sit down to do my dirty business,
my gonads hangle [sic] in the water."

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:26.680
"Hangle?"

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:27.190
"Sorry about that.

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:29.739
'They hangle in the water!'"

00:03:29.739 --> 00:03:30.739
Thanks for watching!

00:03:30.739 --> 00:03:33.610
